+# Editorial review — Movement 9 (Chapters 55–60)
+
+## Status and verdict
+
+This is a **separate-model informed editorial review** of all six supplied chapters in declared order, with the compiled canon, ledger, movement packet, protected-wording map, formula instructions, measured metrics, and source-overlap report. It is not author self-review, not a blind fresh-context read, and not a real human audience test.
+
+**Verdict: REPAIR REQUIRED, bounded.** The movement is structurally and emotionally successful, and the binding ending state is intact. It does, however, contain one direct Tier A wording violation and two runs that the supplied overlap report classifies as unprotected source reuse. Those are release blockers under the brief. Two small clarity repairs would improve one-pass chronology and action staging; no broad rewrite, scene deletion, or fight compression is warranted.
+
+## Unscored reader response
+
+Interest catches immediately in Chapter 55: Bracken's clock, four leaves under the lamp, and the mismatch between four light forms and the eleven-pound answer make procedure feel dangerous before any explanation can take over. It strengthens through the four distinct departures: Lira writes herself out in her own words, Brom refuses a future the frame has honestly found for him, Karis surrenders the institutional scaffolding around her work without surrendering her tier, and Cael receives the margin that says nobody pushed him.
+
+The people who matter most in this movement are Lira and Seln. Lira's “Same door. Same leaving. Mine this time.” condenses her whole institutional arc into a choice she owns. Seln's true-but-empty reports, bad backward look from the crest, prepared fire, and willingness to sleep when his own pupils out-argue him turn a watcher into the fifth traveler by acts rather than confession. Brom and Karis remain independent centers of care: he carries, checks, and refuses to read the family request; she turns lost privileges into the first archive nobody can close.
+
+Interest slips briefly in Chapter 58. The institutional aftermath advances through the week after the academic close, then the narration returns to the party leaving Ostrand on the earlier First-day without explicitly marking the rewind. The opening remains thematically strong, but the reader must reconstruct the time shift. Chapter 60's long inventory also becomes denser than the surrounding prose, though its emotional point—the names, the protected source, and the honest blank—still lands.
+
+I expect the first eastern hold, where five people must price labor and trust without registry categories, and I expect the escalated file to find a new route toward them. I would continue. The book closes its local argument completely while opening a materially different world rather than merely postponing the same hearing.
+
+## Reader lenses
+
+These are editorial simulations, not demographic research or sales predictions.
+
+### Fluent thirteen-year-old lens
+
+| Dimension | Score | Exact passage location | Reason | Confidence |
+|---|---:|---|---|---|
+| Opening pull | 9/10 | Ch. 55, opening through “Four leaves lay in a row under the lamp” | The clock taboo, the waiting group, and four small papers establish a concrete ritual and immediate stakes before explanation. | High |
+| Keep reading | 8/10 | Ch. 57, “He stopped there, and waited to be counted”; Ch. 60, final paragraph | Seln's reunion pays a strong promise, and the final self-definition opens the east and the pursuit. The anonymous-office material delays the reunion longer than some younger readers will prefer. | High |
+| Interest / freshness | 9/10 | Ch. 57, the readers discovering that fifteen years of true reports are “also empty”; Ch. 59, the map key printed over unexplored territory | Paper, records, omission, and literal compliance act as plot mechanisms rather than decoration. The solutions feel specific to these people and this world. | High |
+| Clarity / flow | 7/10 | Ch. 58, from “The machine took a week over it” to “They left Ostrand at first light”; Ch. 60, the nine-fragment inventory | Most physical action reads once, but the unmarked temporal rewind and the inventory's stacked names, secrecy qualifications, and system terms raise the listening load. | High |
+| Character attachment | 9/10 | Ch. 55, “ON MY OWN FEET”; Ch. 56, Rooke's *Late* struck through and replaced by *Hers*; Ch. 58, Seln asking for bread | Agency and vulnerability appear in different forms for different people. Care is expressed through work, records, food, and restraint rather than interchangeable speeches. | High |
+| Humor / warmth | 8/10 | Ch. 58, “Tell it the horse hasn't any” / “He'd be offended”; Ch. 60, Seln “out-argued by my own syllabus” | The humor belongs to character habits and never cancels grief. Bread, cheese, cups, boxes, and shared labor carry warmth cleanly. | High |
+| Action / suspense | 8/10 | Ch. 58, the second ford; Ch. 60, the scree descent | The ford has clear roles, a changed problem, a costly response, and a consequence tied to the boxes. The scree sequence is strong but briefly leaves the other two boxes unplaced. | High |
+| Progression payoff | 8/10 | Ch. 59, the Line giving no answer to either fragment; Ch. 60, the two poles left standing | Progression is restraint and interpretation as well as acquisition: Cael names impulses, refuses opportunistic deployment, and can finally hold two true propositions together. The inventory's terminology is demanding aloud. | Medium-high |
+| Connection | 9/10 | Ch. 55, four forms and five words; Ch. 57, Seln's final return ending in “Five”; Ch. 60, five cups and the anonymous fifth file line | Counts, doors, margins, lamps, records, fires, and boxes recur with changed meanings and carry state cleanly across the six chapters. | High |
+| Read-aloud quality | 8/10 | Ch. 55, Bracken's “hear the hinge” sentence; Ch. 57, Seln on the crest; Ch. 60, Cael's spoken nine-things sentence | Dialogue attribution and cadence are strong. The legal passages and protected inventory are more page-friendly than one-hearing friendly. | High |
